\section{Sorting strata}{


\code{sort_strata} replaces the default ordering of the strata at each axis
(the ordering induced by the values of the \code{stratum} aesthetic) with an
ordering computed from the alluvial structure of the whole panel. It is
applied before \code{decreasing}, \code{reverse}, and \code{absolute}, so those
parameters retain their usual meanings: \code{reverse} still flips the computed
order, and \code{decreasing} still promotes stratum size above it.

\code{sort_strata} must be given the same value in every layer of a plot, just
as \code{decreasing}, \code{reverse}, and \code{absolute} must be: each layer computes
its own positions, so sorting the strata of \code{geom_stratum()} alone would
leave the flows of \code{geom_flow()} at the heights the default order gave
them, detached from the boxes they belong to. Setting the package option
is usually easier than repeating the parameter. Results are cached within
a session, so the computation is still performed only once for a given
panel, engine, and set of arguments.

Some algorithms are stochastic --- wompwomp's \code{"tsp"} is, since the solver
it calls starts from a random tour, while its default \code{"neighbornet"} is
not. The cache is what keeps a stochastic algorithm from ordering the
strata differently in each layer of the same plot, but a \emph{new session}
will still produce a different (equally uncrossed) order. Use a
deterministic algorithm, or \code{set.seed()} before the plot is first drawn,
when a plot must reproduce exactly. \code{\link[=uncross_reset]{uncross_reset()}} empties the cache.

Uncrossing needs to know which strata are linked, so it requires the
\code{alluvium} aesthetic. \code{stat_stratum()} invents one for lodes-form data
that provides none; rather than uncross links that are an artifact of row
order, it warns and leaves the strata alone.
}

\section{Coloring strata}{


\code{color_strata} assigns each stratum an integer cluster identifier, shared
across axes, so that strata containing largely the same cases receive the
same identifier. It is exposed as the computed variable \code{cluster} and is
used via \code{\link[ggplot2:aes_eval]{ggplot2::after_stat()}}:
\code{aes(fill = after_stat(cluster))}. It is a factor, so it maps to a
discrete fill or color scale.

Unlike \code{sort_strata}, \code{color_strata} moves nothing, so it need only be set
in the layers that read \code{after_stat(cluster)}.

On \code{geom_alluvium()} a differentiation aesthetic must be constant along an
alluvium; because \code{cluster} is a property of the stratum, not of the
alluvium, use it with \code{geom_stratum()}, \code{geom_lode()}, or \code{geom_flow()}.
}

\section{Sorting axes}{


The order of the axes themselves cannot be set from within a statistical
transformation: position scales are trained and mapped before stats are
computed, so permuting \code{x} inside a \verb{stat_*()} would move the strata out
from under their axis labels. \code{sort_axes()} is therefore an ordinary
function that returns the recommended axis order, to be passed to
\code{\link[ggplot2:scale_discrete]{ggplot2::scale_x_discrete()}} or to \code{\link[=to_lodes_form]{to_lodes_form()}}.

The \strong{wompwomp} engine's default here is \code{column_method = "tsp"}, which
is stochastic in the same way as \code{sort_strata}'s \code{"tsp"}: repeated calls
may return a tour and its reverse, which are equally uncrossed but order
the axes oppositely. Seed it, or pass
\code{list("wompwomp", method = "neighbornet")}, when the axis order must be
reproducible.

Candidate axis orders are compared by the crossings they induce, which
depends on how the strata are ordered, so the engine sorts the strata
while it searches. That inner ordering is \code{"neighbornet"} by default and
can be set with \code{strata_method}, as in
\code{list("wompwomp", method = "tsp", strata_method = "greedy_wblf")}. Only
the axis order is returned.

Applying the result with \code{scale_x_discrete(limits = )} changes the \code{x}
positions that the \verb{stat_*()} layers see, and it does so before they run,
so \code{sort_strata} sorts the strata against the new axis order rather than
the original one. This is what you want --- crossings occur between
adjacent axes, so the best stratum order depends on which axes are
adjacent --- but it does mean the strata of a plot with a reordered axis
scale will not match those of the same plot without it. Sorting the axes
and then the strata is also two passes over a problem that
\code{\link[wompwomp:sort_to_uncross]{wompwomp::sort_to_uncross()}} can optimize jointly; call it directly on
your data when you want the joint result.
